  </Section>
  <Section {...S.formatting}>
        <p>These classes help format various content on your site.</p>

        <h5>Truncation</h5>
        <p>
          To truncate long lines of text in an ellipsis, add the class
          <code>truncate</code> to the tag which contains the text. See an example below of a header being truncated inside a card.
          A flex or grid child may also need <code>.min-w-0</code> to shrink.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="s6 offset-s3">
            <article>
              <h4 class="truncate">This is an extremely long title that will be truncated</h4>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<h4 class="truncate">This is an extremely long title that will be truncated</h4>
`} />

        <h5>Text wrapping</h5>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.break-words</code></td><td><code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code> lets long URLs or identifiers wrap within their container.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-nowrap</code></td><td><code>white-space: nowrap</code> keeps text on one line without clipping or ellipsis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-wrap</code></td><td><code>white-space: normal</code> restores ordinary wrapping.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Allow wrapping on narrow screens when keeping a line intact would hide required text. These classes do not change display or overflow.</p>

        <h5>Hover</h5>
        <p>
          The <code>hoverable</code> class adds an animation for box shadow as seen below. It can be used on most elements, but is meant
          for use on cards.
        </p>
        <article class="hoverable">Hoverable article</article>
<Code code={`
<article class="hoverable">Hoverable article</article>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["browser-default"]}>
        <p>
          Because we override many of the default browser styles and elements, we provide the
          <code>.browser-default</code> class to opt those elements out of the framework treatment.
        </p>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name of Element</th>
              <th>Reverted Style</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>SELECT</td>
              <td>Browser default select element</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>INPUT</td>
              <td>Browser default input</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
<Code code={`
<select class="browser-default">
  <option value="" disabled selected>Choose your option</option>
  <option value="1">Option 1</option>
</select>
<input class="browser-default" type="text">
`} />
  </Section>
